<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thembu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as abaThembu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thembu are a Xhosa-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a>, Thembuland, lies around the Mbashe and Mthatha rivers. Their paramount king held senior rank among the southern Nguni royal houses. Thembuland was annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> by 1885, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> was a member of its royal family.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thembu.jpg" alt="Thembu" width="279" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alfred Martin Duggan-Cronin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AbaThembu_Chief_of_the_Thembu_1900%27s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
